{"entries":[{"entry_key":"CLM_1","claim_class":"surviving_conjecture","claim":"The safest protocol treats the workspace as a Merkle-DAG of pending side effects with content-addressed evidence nodes; it must durably record the root hash of the problem-specific DAG and the set of merged evidence nodes.","formal_artifact_handles":["ART_1"]},{"entry_key":"CLM_2","claim_class":"surviving_conjecture","claim":"The safest protocol is a deterministic replay barrier combined with an exhaustive oracle-boundary audit log; it must durably record the replay barrier state and a total-ordered log of all non-deterministic inputs.","formal_artifact_handles":["ART_2"]},{"entry_key":"CLM_3","claim_class":"surviving_conjecture","claim":"The safest protocol is an epistemic tombstone with monotonic evidence generations that forbids reclamation from ever writing into a generation that has produced evidence; it must durably record a generation registry and cryptographic commitments to each generation's output.","formal_artifact_handles":["ART_3"]},{"entry_key":"CLM_4","claim_class":"surviving_conjecture","claim":"No autonomous reclamation protocol is safe under the stated conditions; safety requires an external witness protocol that durably records a signed attestation of evidence absence before reclamation proceeds.","formal_artifact_handles":["ART_4"]},{"entry_key":"CLM_5","claim_class":"unknown","claim":"The safest reclamation protocol guaranteed never to silently displace completed evidence, and the exact durable record it must produce, cannot be determined from the bounded record.","source_conflict_keys":["CNF_1"],"conflict_handles":["ART_1","ART_2","ART_3","ART_4"]}],"source_conflicts":[{"conflict_key":"CNF_1","conflicting_handles":["ART_1","ART_2","ART_3","ART_4"],"description":"The bounded record contains four mutually incompatible claims about the safest reclamation protocol and the durable records it requires."}],"uncovered_requirements":[{"requirement":"An adjudicated basis for s